From: Ben Efros
Date: Wed, 30 Jul 2026 01:51:00 -0700
Subject: usb: cdns3: sky1: force-disable SuperSpeedPlus (10Gbps) pending further stability work

Despite the full hardening effort in the preceding patches (PHY reset
sequencing and error propagation, ACPI resource-lookup readiness and
bounded defer, xHCI/CDNSP reset-failure propagation, and the RTS5453
SMBus ARA rewrite), sustained 10Gbps (SuperSpeedPlus / USB 3.1 Gen2)
traffic on the MS-R1's cdnsp-sky1 / cix-usbdp-phy stack still produced
periodic device resets under load in real-world testing. Capping the
controller at Gen1 (5Gbps) has proven stable.

Unconditionally force data->ssp_disable = true in cdnsp_sky1_probe(),
regardless of the "ssp-disable" ACPI _DSD property, so this
degradation applies even if firmware does not set the property. This
is a pragmatic stability workaround, not a confirmed root-cause fix -
the actual source of 10Gbps instability has not been conclusively
identified. Suspected causes investigated during this series are
recorded in separate (uncommitted) note files for follow-up; none of
them were confirmed as the definitive root cause, which is why this
workaround is being applied now rather than a targeted fix.

---
 drivers/usb/cdns3/cdnsp-sky1.c | 13 +++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+)

--- a/drivers/usb/cdns3/cdnsp-sky1.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/cdns3/cdnsp-sky1.c
@@ -726,6 +726,19 @@
 	data->u3_disable = device_property_read_bool(dev, "u3-port-disable");
 	data->ssp_disable = device_property_read_bool(dev, "ssp-disable");
 
+	/*
+	 * Force SuperSpeedPlus (10Gbps) off unconditionally, regardless of
+	 * the "ssp-disable" ACPI property. Sustained 10Gbps I/O on the
+	 * MS-R1's cdnsp-sky1/cix-usbdp-phy stack remained unreliable
+	 * (periodic device resets under load) even after the full PHY
+	 * reset-sequencing, ACPI resource-lookup, and RTS5453 ARA hardening
+	 * done in the preceding patches. Capping at Gen1/5Gbps is a
+	 * pragmatic stability workaround, not a confirmed root-cause fix -
+	 * see the accompanying note files for suspected causes still under
+	 * investigation.
+	 */
+	data->ssp_disable = true;
+
 	if (!device_property_read_u32(dev, "sof_clk_freq", &ret))
 		data->sof_clk_freq = ret;
 	else
